Best Practices for Imaging with Winget
Avoid running winget packages that deploy as per-user MSIX/AppX files during your reference image build.
If you must use winget, ensure installations are forced system-wide, or perform application installations via traditional enterprise installers (MSI/EXE) in Audit Mode rather than using user-context winget commands.
Clean up any updated AppX/Store packages or remove the Desktop App Installer package if Sysprep throws an app-package validation error, noting that winget can be reinstalled post-deployment. 
If you're hitting a specific error code or log entry in setupact.log, share it so I can help you troubleshoot the exact package causing the failure.




To mass install applications before running Sysprep without breaking the image, you should use enterprise-grade deployment tools that install software system-wide (per-machine) rather than per-user.
The most reliable alternatives and methods for mass installation in a reference image include:
1. Ninite Pro or Silent Command-Line Scripts
Instead of winget, you can use Ninite Pro or a batch/PowerShell script that triggers the silent switches of official enterprise installers (MSI or EXE).
Why it works: MSI installers naturally deploy to Program Files and register for all users, which Sysprep fully supports.
How to use: Boot your reference machine into Audit Mode (Ctrl+Shift+F3 at the OOBE screen) and run a script utilizing silent switches (e.g., setup.exe /silent or msiexec /i package.msi /qn).
2. Chocolatey (Open-Source Package Manager)
Chocolatey is a command-line package manager for Windows similar to winget, but it is much safer for Sysprep because it prioritizes traditional machine-wide installers over modern UWP/AppX packages.
Why it works: It primarily fetches standard .msi and .exe installers and runs them with silent arguments under the system context.
How to use: Install Chocolatey in Audit Mode, then mass-install your suite with a single command:
powershell
choco install googlechrome vlc 7zip git -y
Use code with caution.
3. PowerShell App Deployment Toolkit (PSADT)
PSADT is a highly customizable framework used by enterprise administrators to bundle and mass-deploy applications.
Why it works: It allows you to chain multiple installations together, handle dependencies, and force strict per-machine install rules.
How to use: You create a master deployment script using their syntax and execute it in Audit Mode to install your software catalog sequentially.
4. DISM (Deployment Image Servicing and Management)
If you want to inject applications directly into your Windows image offline (without even booting the machine), you can use DISM.
Why it works: It bypasses the live Windows environment entirely, meaning there is zero risk of user-profile corruption or app ownership conflicts.
How to use: This is primarily used for provisioning AppX/MSIX packages properly so they apply to all future users, or for installing features-on-demand and updates:
powershell
Dism /Image:C:\mount\windows /Add-ProvisionedAppxPackage /PackagePath:C:\apps\package.msix
Use code with caution.
Crucial Golden Rules for Mass Installing
Always use Audit Mode: Do not go through the initial Windows setup wizard to install your software. Press Ctrl+Shift+F3 at the region selection screen to log into the built-in Administrator account.
Avoid the Microsoft Store: Do not launch or let the Microsoft Store update apps in the background while installing your software, as this is the #1 cause of Sysprep breaks.
Are you managing these deployments using a specific framework like MDT (Microsoft Deployment Toolkit), SCCM/MECM, or are you building a standalone ISO/WIM manually? Knowing your setup will help narrow down the best tool.
